+# Ground Zero cross: appellate account of a museum transfer
+
+STL01–STL03 / AUD011. Source inspected 2026-11-15T15:17:28.488516+00:00. A federal appellate opinion supplies a specific documentary lead concerning one recovered steel artifact and a 2011 museum transfer. It is not a chain-of-custody inventory for all WTC steel.
+
+## Source observations
+
+The [Second Circuit opinion, *American Atheists, Inc. v. Port Authority*, No. 13-1668, July 28, 2014](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca2-13-01668/pdf/USCOURTS-ca2-13-01668-0.pdf#page=11), physical PDF page 11 (opinion p. 11), describes a column and crossbeam temporarily housed at St. Peter’s Church until 2011. It says the Port Authority transferred custody of that artifact and **“other Ground Zero artifacts”** to the September 11 Memorial and Museum Foundation. Page 10 describes an initial plan for an airport hangar and the decision to house the cross temporarily at the church. The opinion’s background is a judicial account for a display dispute, not a specimen-level transfer manifest.
+
+A [legal-reporter rendering](https://openjurist.org/760/f3d/227/american-atheists-inc-v-port-authority) displays the same passage with `*235` immediately before “other Ground Zero artifacts.” Preserve that exact formatting alongside the government-hosted opinion, which shows “other Ground Zero artifacts” without a numeric count in this sentence. **This review does not establish 235 as a count of transferred artifacts.** Whether the asterisked figure is a reporter page marker is an untested formatting hypothesis; obtain the publisher’s pagination convention or an authenticated reporter page to test it. Do not silently normalize either representation.
+
+## Limits and next records
+
+The opinion does not provide the transfer manifest, full item count, measurements, origin certification, or an inventory linking W14A/W14B or any NIST-tested specimen to this transfer. It does not establish loss, concealment, fabrication or wrongdoing. Its passage about the hangar is a plan and the opinion separately reports temporary church custody; this review does not assert that this particular artifact entered the hangar.
